For the link, `__strcspn_c1` is exported by libc.so.6 but maybe the linker needs the symbol version. The reference `U __strcspn_c1` (unversioned UND) should bind to the versioned definition... Normally that works. Unless the linker script... Let me check what happens with a tiny test. Alternatively, define our own __strcspn_c1 in driver.cc like the other shim. But careful: defining a symbol that libc also has. Since it's a normal function (not ifunc), defining our own in the executable will just override/satisfy. Let me implement it via strcspn.

Actually the real reason: when linking against libc.so.6, the symbol `__strcspn_c1` might be a *compat* symbol available only at runtime but not at link time... It's marked with `(GLIBC_2.2.5)` default version at runtime, but the link-time stub libc.so might not include it (compat symbols are excluded from the link-time libc.so). That's exactly it: these are compat symbols removed from the link-time stubs in newer glibc.

Simplest: provide our own implementation in a shim C file. The linker will prefer our definition. Add to driver.cc:

